## 7. Verify the KV v2 Configuration

The mount configuration was inspected with:

```bash
vault read sys/mounts/secret
```

### Actual terminal output

```text
options                    map[version:2]
type                       kv
```

This confirms that the `secret/` mount uses the KV secrets engine with version 2 enabled.

## 8. Verify the Secret Exists

The secret was checked without displaying the password:

```bash
vault kv get -field=admin_password secret/mlflow >/dev/null \
  && echo "PASS: admin_password exists and is non-empty"
```

### Actual terminal output

```text
PASS: admin_password exists and is non-empty
```

## 9. Verify MLflow Startup

The startup wrapper was given time to detect the new Vault secret and start MLflow.

```bash
for i in $(seq 1 12); do
  if curl -fsS -o /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:5000/; then
    echo "PASS: MLflow is responding on port 5000"
    break
  fi
  sleep 5
done
```

### Actual terminal output

```text
PASS: MLflow is responding on port 5000
```

The HTTP endpoint was then checked directly:

```bash
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code}\n' \
  http://127.0.0.1:5000/
```

### Actual terminal output

```text
HTTP 200
```

An HTTP 200 response confirms that the MLflow web endpoint was responding successfully.

